"surface":"களத்து","parts":[{"text":"களத்து","lemma":"களத்து","gloss":"on the battlefield"}],"gloss":"on the battlefield","split_unverified":true},{"surface":"ஒழிய","parts":[{"text":"ஒழிய","lemma":"ஒழி","gloss":"to perish"}],"gloss":"to perish"}]},{"words":[{"surface":"அருஞ்","parts":[{"text":"அரு","lemma":"அருமை","gloss":"difficult"},{"text":"ஞ்","lemma":"ஞ்","gloss":"sandhi consonant"}],"gloss":"difficult"},{"surface":"சமம்","parts":[{"text":"சமம்","lemma":"சமம்","gloss":"battle"}],"gloss":"battle"},{"surface":"ததையத்","parts":[{"text":"ததைய","lemma":"ததை","gloss":"to shatter"},{"text":"த்","lemma":"த்","gloss":"sandhi consonant"}],"gloss":"to shatter"},{"surface":"தாக்கி","parts":[{"text":"தாக்கி","lemma":"தாக்கு","gloss":"having attacked"}],"gloss":"having attacked"},{"surface":"நன்றும்","parts":[{"text":"நன்றும்","lemma":"நன்று","gloss":"greatly"}],"gloss":"greatly"}]},{"words":[{"surface":"நண்ணாத்","parts":[{"text":"நண்ணா","lemma":"நண்ணு","gloss":"unapproaching/hostile"},{"text":"த்","lemma":"த்","gloss":"sandhi consonant"}],"gloss":"hostile"},{"surface":"தெவ்வர்த்","parts":[{"text":"தெவ்வர்","lemma":"தெவ்வர்","gloss":"enemies"},{"text":"த்","lemma":"த்","gloss":"sandhi consonant"}],"gloss":"enemies"},{"surface":"தாங்கும்","parts":[{"text":"தாங்கும்","lemma":"தாங்கு","gloss":"resisting"}],"gloss":"resisting"}]},{"words":[{"surface":"பெண்ணையம்","parts":[{"text":"பெண்ணை","lemma":"பெண்ணை","gloss":"Pennai river"},{"text":"அம்","lemma":"அம்","gloss":"beautiful/adjective suffix"}],"gloss":"Pennai river"},{"surface":"படப்பை","parts":[{"text":"படப்பை","lemma":"படப்பை","gloss":"fertile garden"}],"gloss":"fertile garden"},{"surface":"நாடுகிழ","parts":[{"text":"நாடு","lemma":"நாடு","gloss":"land"},{"text":"கிழ","lemma":"கிழவோன்","gloss":"lord"}],"gloss":"lord of the land"},{"surface":"வோயே","parts":[{"text":"வோயே","lemma":"வோன்","gloss":"oh lord!"}],"gloss":"oh lord!"}]}],"literal":"Having taken the gold from the forehead plates of enemies' elephants, to make the bards' heads shine / having adorned them with unfading golden lotuses of great glory, / O descendant of the strong man of unretreating determination! / Even though we are not capable, quickly / if we speak of your qualities, like the night / dense darkness where sleep has folded in the thicket, / the warriors of Muḷḷūr hill with waterfalls sounding like drums, / along with your relatives of indestructible lineage, may you flourish! / Upon the earth, to all the spread-out people, / Kapilar, the wise Brahmin free of sensory blemishes, / sang so that there is no space left now for the begging people, / and his fame spread; because of that, / when the Chera king of anger-abundant army sailed gold-bringing ships / in the western sea, other ships could not go there; / we are just like those other ships! / Driven by poverty, coming in distress, / we bound ourselves to your generosity. Like the thunder / that strikes the sharp-toothed serpent, when the war drums rise and sound, / leaving the majestic elephants and kings dead on the battlefield, / attacking so that the difficult battle shatters, / you hold back the hostile enemies, / O lord of the land of fertile gardens along the Pennai river!","translation":"O descendant of the strong-willed ancestor who took gold from the forehead plates of enemy elephants to fashion unfading golden lotuses for the heads of bards! Even if we lack the skill, if we sing of you, may you flourish with your indestructible kin at Muḷḷūr hill, where waterfalls roar like drums amidst thickets dark as sleep. The spotless poet Kapilar sang of you so bountifully that no room remains for any more seekers, and his fame has spread everywhere. Just as other ships cannot sail where the Chera king's gold-bearing fleet dominates the western sea, we find ourselves unable to praise you further, for Kapilar has said it all. Yet, driven by poverty, we have come to seek your renowned generosity. O lord of the fertile lands watered by the Pennai river, who shatters the enemy in fierce battles, leaving kings and their majestic elephants dead on the field while your war drums thunder like serpent-striking lightning!","modern":"பகைவர்களுடைய யானைகளின் நெற்றிப் பட்டத்துத் தங்கத்தைக் கொண்டு, பாணர்களின் தலைகள் விளங்கும்படி வாடாத பொற்றாமரையைச் சூட்டிய சிறந்த பெருமையுடைய, போரில் பின்வாங்காத வலிமைமிக்க முன்னோரின் வழித்தோன்றலே! நாங்கள் பாடும் திறமை இல்லாதவர்கள் என்றாலும், உன்னைப் பாடினால், முரசு போல ஒலிக்கும் அருவிகளைக் கொண்ட முள்ளூர் மலையின் தலைவனே, உன்னுடைய அழியாத சுற்றத்தாருடன் நீ டூழி வாழ்க! உலகத்தில் உள்ள எல்லா மக்களுக்கும் கேட்கும்படி, குற்றமற்ற அறிவையுடைய கபிலர் உன்னைப் பாடிப் புகழ் பெற்றார். அதனால், இரப்பவர்களுக்கு இனிமேல் இடம் இல்லாதவாறு உனது புகழ் பரவியது. சினமிக்க சேர மன்னன் தன் கப்பல்களை மேற்குக்கடலில் செலுத்தியபோது பிற கப்பல்கள் அங்கே செல்ல முடியாதவாறு தடுத்தது போல, கபிலர் உன்னைப் பாடி முடித்ததால் எங்களைப் போன்ற பிற பாணர்கள் பாடுவதற்கு வழியில்லாமல் தவிக்கிறோம். ஆயினும், வறுமை எங்களைத் துரத்துவதால் உனது கொடைத்தன்மையை நாடி வந்துள்ளோம். பெண்ணையாற்றின் கரையில் உள்ள செழிப்பான நாட்டின் தலைவனே! இடி முழக்கம் போல முரசுகள் ஒலிக்க, பகை வேந்தர்களும் அவர்களின் யானைகளும் போர்க்களத்தில் மடியும்படி தாக்கி வெல்லும் வீரனே!","explanation":"This poem is addressed to Malaiyamāṉ Tirumuṭikkāri by the poet Māṟōkkattu Nappacalaiyār. The poet praises Kāri's lineage, noting how his ancestors adorned bards with golden lotuses made from the spoils of enemy elephants. The poet then references the great poet Kapilar, who has already sung of Kāri so extensively and received so much patronage that there seems to be no room left for other bards to praise him. This is compared to the Chera king's ships dominating the western sea, preventing other vessels from sailing. Despite this, driven by poverty, the poet has come to seek Kāri's legendary generosity. The poem beautifully blends the themes of war (puram) and patronage, highlighting the fertile landscape of the Pennai river and the strength of the chief.","model":"gemini-3.5-flash"},"timing":{"male":[[0.0,0.4]